ility actions, \"evidential blackmail\"), and flags which claims come from the standard literature and which are my own interpretive proposals.\n\n## 2. The formal definition\n\nFollowing Gibbard and Harper (1976), the evidential expected utility of an action $A$ is\n\n$$\nV(A) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j} P(O_j \\mid A)\\, D(O_j),\n$$\n\nwhere $O_1, \\dots, O_n$ are a partition of the possible outcomes, $D(O_j)$ is the desirability (utility) of outcome $O_j$, and $P(O_j \\mid A)$ is the agent's conditional credence that $O_j$ obtains, given that she performs $A$. The recommendation is: **perform the action maximizing $V(A)$.**\n\nContrast the causal expected utility:\n\n$$\nU(A) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j} P(A \\;\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\; O_j)\\, D(O_j),\n$$\n\nwhere $P(A \\,\\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow\\, O_j)$ is the agent's credence in the *counterfactual* \"if $A$ were performed, $O_j$ would obtain.\" The indicative conditional probability $P(O_j \\mid A)$ and the counterfactual probability $P(A \\Box\\!\\!\\rightarrow O_j)$ are genuinely different quantities. The indicative conditional is symmetrical in a way the counterfactual is not: my taking one box is evidence about the box's contents, and the box's contents are evidence about what I will take, even though neither causes the other. Gibbard and Harper's insight was that all disagreements between EDT and CDT trace to exactly this gap.\n\n**A graph-theoretic gloss (my own framing, though standard in spirit).** It helps to think of the decision problem as a Bayes net over variables including the agent's disposition/decision procedure $T$, the action $A$, and state variables $S$ (including payoff-relevant facts). EDT evaluates $A$ by conditioning: $P(O \\mid A)$ reflects *all* statistical dependence between $A$ and $O$, including dependence routed through common causes like $T$. CDT evaluates $A$ by surgery on the graph: cut all incoming causal arrows into $A$ (treating $A$ as an intervention) and recompute. EDT says: you are a node in the network like any other, and your action is information. CDT says: at the moment of choice, only downstream effects matter.\n\n## 3. Newcomb's problem\n\nTwo boxes are on offer. Box A transparently contains \\$1,000. Box B contains \\$1,000,000 or nothing, set by a superb predictor: if the predictor *predicted* you would take only B, B contains \\$1,000,000; if the predictor predicted you would take both, B is empty. The prediction was made before your choice; the predictor is right 99% of the time. You may take only B, or take both.\n\nThe setup is engineered so that your action and the box's contents are strongly correlated without any causal link running either way — the correlation is generated by their common cause, the predictor's model of you.\n\nEDT's calculation (with the source's numbers) is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{take B}) &= P(\\text{filled} \\mid \\text{take B})\\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000 + P(\\text{empty} \\mid \\text{take B}) \\cdot \\$0 \\\\\n&= 0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000{,}000 = \\$990{,}000,\\\\[4pt]\nV(\\text{take both}) &= P(\\text{filled} \\mid \\text{both})\\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000 + P(\\text{empty} \\mid \\text{both}) \\cdot \\$1{,}000 \\\\\n&= 0.01 \\cdot \\$1{,}001{,}000 + 0.99 \\cdot \\$1{,}000 = \\$11{,}000.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking one box is *news* that the predictor marked you as a one-boxer, hence that the million is there. EDT recommends one box. CDT, evaluating counterfactuals in which your present choice cannot reach back and alter an already-made prediction, recommends two boxes: causally, two-boxing dominates, adding \\$1,000 whatever B contains.\n\nEDT's one-boxing is often greeted as a reductio. Two responses. First, the \"news value\" reading is coherent: if you most want to learn you'll be rich, the best news you can receive is that you are the kind of agent who one-boxes. Second, and more substantively (a point I develop, in the spirit of Wolpert and Benford 2013): the paradox's payoff structure means that in a population of EDT agents, one-boxers walk away with \\$1,000,000 and two-boxers with \\$1,000. If rationality is judged by the results achieved by the *agents who possess it*, not by the local merits of each reflex, EDT looks vindicated. This is contentious — the two-boxer says the one-boxer is rich but irrational, the way someone who believes he'll win the lottery feels lucky. But it motivates a policy-level reading of EDT to which I return in §7.\n\n## 4. The twin prisoner's dilemma\n\nAomame and her psychological twin — an agent whose reasoning is functionally identical to hers — are seated in separate rooms, each choosing to cooperate or defect. Payoffs to Aomame (symmetrically to her twin): mutual cooperation \\$5, mutual defection \\$1, unilateral cooperation \\$0, unilateral defection \\$10. Classical game theory, treating the twin's move as independent, recommends defection.\n\nEDT reasons otherwise. Aomame's own deliberation is the best evidence she will ever get of her twin's deliberation, because they are the *same computation*. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV(\\text{cooperate}) &= P(\\text{twin C} \\mid \\text{Aomame C})\\cdot \\$5 + P(\\text{twin D} \\mid \\text{Aomame C}) \\cdot \\$0 = \\$5,\\\\\nV(\\text{defect}) &= P(\\text{twin C} \\mid \\text{Aomame D})\\cdot \\$10 + P(\\text{twin D} \\mid \\text{Aomame D}) \\cdot \\$1 = \\$1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEDT recommends cooperation. Note this is not altruism — Aomame cares only about her own payout — but *evidential* reasoning: defecting is news that her twin defects, and a defecting twin costs her more than the unilateral gain is worth.\n\nThis connects to Hofstadter's and Doyle's notion of **superrationality**: when choosing, treat your decision as correlated with the decisions of all agents running \"the same\" decision procedure. EDT generalizes the idea: the action one evidentially evaluates includes its correlation with *any* correlated decision-makers, not just identical twins — other humans, other copies of an AI system, other agents that have modeled your reasoning.\n\nA partial-decomposition caveat (my own development of the source's calculation): the idealized computation above assumes perfect correlation, $P(\\text{twin C}\\mid \\text{C}) = 1$. With noisy correlation $\\rho$, one gets $V(\\text{C}) = \\$5\\rho + \\$0(1-\\rho)$ versus $V(\\text{D}) = \\$10(1-\\rho') + \\$1\\rho'$ (where $\\rho' = P(\\text{twin C}\\mid \\text{D})$), so cooperation is recommended whenever $5\\rho > 10(1-\\rho') + \\rho'$ — i.e., whenever the twin-correlation is strong relative to the temptation. Real agents are imperfect copies; EDT's verdict is parameter-dependent in exactly the way one should expect.\n\n## 5. Where EDT's machinery needs care\n\n### 5.1 The conditioning problem and \"managing the news\"\n\nDavid Lewis (1981) famously charged that EDT promotes \"an irrational policy of managing the news\": it values actions for the good headlines they generate rather than the good outcomes they produce. James Joyce (1999) echoed: rational agents act on *causal efficacy*, not *auspiciousness*.\n\nThe charge is sharpened by the **smoking lesion** problem (Eells 1982; Sober 1984), a case the Wikipedia source does not treat but which every serious evaluation of EDT must face. Suppose a gene causes both a desire to smoke and lung cancer; the gene is the only source of the correlation, and smoking has no causal effect on cancer either way. An agent who values smoking should smoke: causally it is free and pleasant. But EDT computes\n\n$$\nV(\\text{smoke}) = P(\\text{cancer}\\mid \\text{smoke})\\,D(\\cdot) \\;\\gg\\; V(\\text{abstain}),\n$$\n\nbecause smoking is excellent evidence that one carries the gene. If the agent's utilities are set so that avoiding cancer is worth more than the pleasure of smoking, EDT recommends abstaining — pointlessly, since by hypothesis smoking doesn't affect whether she has cancer. She is managing the news.\n\nHow might an evidentialist respond? Several lines exist in the literature, and I'll state my own operational view.\n\n- **Eells' \"tickle\" defense.** The correlation between action and gene runs *through the agent's introspective evidence*: what she smokes for is her desire, and she already knows her desires at decision time. Given her full introspective state, $P(\\text{cancer} \\mid \\text{smoke}, \\text{desire}) = P(\\text{cancer} \\mid \\text{desire})$, and the evidential advantage of smoking evaporates. Formally: EDT should condition on the agent's *total evidence at decision time*, including her knowledge of her own dispositions, not on the bare act. This is principled — a rational agent's credence function should already reflect everything she knows, including her own likely choice — but it requires the choice of a conditioning set $\\mathcal{E}$, and on which set to condition is exactly the contested question.\n\n- **My operational proposal.** The cleanest formulation, in my view, is: *EDT evaluates $A$ relative to the agent's epistemic state just before deliberation resolves, i.e., $P(O \\mid A, \\mathcal{E})$ where $\\mathcal{E}$ includes everything the agent knows about herself except the deliberative output itself.* In the smoking lesion, the agent who knows \"I have an urge to smoke, and urges track the gene\" has already updated on the gene-relevant evidence; the marginal act adds no information, so $V(\\text{smoke}) > V(\\text{abstain})$ correctly, matching CDT. In Newcomb, by contrast, the predictor's model of the agent is causally downstream of nothing the agent can introspect at decision time — the agent *cannot* observe her own future choice — so conditioning on the act genuinely changes her credence about the box. On this reading, EDT's scope of evidential relevance is delimited by the agent's own epistemic access, and the smoking lesion simply fails to generate a Newcomb-like correlation *given the agent's information*. This is a substantive interpretive commitment of mine, not something the source asserts; it collapses into Eells' tickle defense under a suitable formalization, and its cost is that it makes EDT's verdicts sensitive to how finely we model self-knowledge.\n\n### 5.2 Zero-probability actions\n\n$P(O_j \\mid A)$ is undefined when $P(A) = 0$. This matters more than it looks. In Newcomb, an agent who is certain she will one-box assigns $P(\\text{two-box}) = 0$ and cannot even evaluate the alternative. Remedies in the literature include infinitesimal probabilities (Jeffrey) and limits: define $V(A) = \\lim_{\\epsilon \\to 0} \\sum_j P(O_j \\mid A, P(A)=\\epsilon) D(O_j)$. My operational recommendation: treat $V(A)$ as the limit of conditioning on a small exogenous *shove* toward $A$ — literally an intervention that makes $A$ marginally more likely without changing anything else. This \"evidential wiggle\" preserves the correlations one wants (the shove is independent of the predictor's past prediction, so $P(\\text{filled} \\mid \\text{shove toward one-box})$ still reads off the correlation) while keeping all conditionals well-defined. I flag this as a repair of my own, in the same spirit as proposals by Eells and by later formalizers.\n\n### 5.3 Evidential blackmail\n\nWorst of all for EDT are cases where an adversary *engineers* auspiciousness. In evidential blackmail (a cousin of cases discussed by Ahmed), a blackmailer who knows whether your house is insured-and-safe or doomed offers to reveal (or fix) bad news for a fee *only if* your house is doomed. Paying is strong evidence your house is doomed — bad news — so EDT refuses to pay. But here refusal does not make the news better; the doom, if real, is already fixed. One can build versions where EDT's \"don't buy bad news\" policy is predictably exploited. This is, in my judgment, the strongest argument that pure conditioning is incomplete as a general decision procedure, and it is why contemporary evidentialists often retreat to the policy-level view discussed next.\n\n## 6. The evidentialist's wager\n\nA pragmatic argument for EDT (MacAskill, Vallinder, Österheld, Shulman, Treutlein 2021) runs as follows. Even if you assign low credence to EDT being *correct*, there are circumstances in which you should *act as if* it were true. The reason: EDT-relevant correlations (your action correlating with many other agents running similar reasoning) can multiply stakes enormously. If a thousand agents in symmetric positions all face the same choice, and your choice is evidence for theirs, the evidential expected value of a cooperative action aggregates across all thousand payoffs you care about (or across copies of yourself). The two theories agree in \"normal\" cases; they diverge precisely where the number of correlated agents is large; so conditioning your *divergent* choices on EDT has high expected value even at moderate credence in EDT. This is a decision-theoretic argument *for* adopting evidential reasoning — itself arguably an EDT-style wager — and it illustrates the theory's most distinctive practical implication: in a world of correlated agents (identical AI instances, predictable humans, genetic relatives), auspiciousness is cheap to acquire and often valuable.\n\n## 7. Acting, policies, and the level of evaluation\n\nOne recurring ambiguity in EDT as standardly presented deserves explicit treatment: *what* is the object being evaluated — the token act, or the policy?\n\nThe Gibbard–Harper formula evaluates token acts. But the deepest defense of EDT's Newcomb and twin verdicts is at the policy level: an agent who *commits in advance* to one-boxing, or to cooperating with her twin, ends up richer. The predictor fills the box *because* she is the kind of agent who one-boxes; the twin cooperates *because* they are the same computation. On this reading, EDT's act-level conditioning is a decision-time approximation of the right thing to evaluate, namely the decision procedure itself: choose the procedure $T$ maximizing $\\mathbb{E}[U \\mid T]$.\n\nThis reading (a development I am proposing; the source presents the act-level theory only, though Ahmed and the \"Evidentialist's Wager\" authors point toward it) has two virtues. First, it explains why EDT one-boxes without invoking any spooky action-at-a-distance: committing to one-boxing causally (via the predictor's model of you) causes the box to be filled. Second, it clarifies the smoking lesion: at the policy level, \"be an agent who smokes when she wants to\" does not cause cancer, so the policy is correctly evaluated favorably. The residual danger — evidential blackmail — then becomes a warning that conditioning on one's own policy choice can still be exploited when adversaries select their offers *conditioned on* your policy. Whether policy-level EDT survives blackmail is an open problem; my suggestion is that the policy formulation should condition on the *procedure*, not on \"news of the procedure,\" which blocks the blackmailer's channel.\n\n## 8. Sleeping Beauty, briefly\n\nEDT has also been deployed in the Sleeping Beauty problem, where a subject awakens (perhaps twice) after a coin flip and must assign credence to heads. It has been argued that halfers who adopt EDT avoid Dutch books (consistency-loss betting schemes), while Conitzer (2015) constructed Dutch books that still catch evidentialist halfers. The case illustrates a general feature: EDT couples one's credences and one's betting policy tightly, and the coupling is exactly where Dutch-book arguments attack. I note it here mainly to show EDT's reach beyond choice problems into epistemology proper; the literature remains unsettled.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\n\nEvidential decision theory replaces the question \"what will my action *bring about*?\" with \"what would it *tell me* if I learned I were doing it?\" Formally, it maximizes\n\n$$\nV(A) = \\sum_j P(O_j \\mid A)\\, D(O_j),\n$$\n\nusing conditional rather than counterfactual probabilities, and it diverges from causal decision theory exactly when actions are correlated with their environments through common causes rather than causal links.\n\nIts verdicts are striking: one-box in Newcomb, cooperate with your twin, and — in the evidentialist's wager — treat your choices as evidence for the choices of all agents who reason as you do. Its pathologies are equally instructive: the smoking lesion shows conditioning can mistake auspiciousness for efficacy, and evidential blackmail shows adversaries can manufacture auspiciousness. My own reading, offered as an interpretation rather than a report of consensus, is that EDT is best formulated with (i) conditioning on the agent's full evidence at decision time, including self-knowledge (defusing the lesion), and (ii) evaluation at the level of decision procedures rather than token acts (grounding the Newcomb and twin verdicts causally). On that reading, EDT is not a rival causality to CDT but a rival *unit of evaluation* — and in a world where agents are correlated, models, and are modeled, the evidential unit may be the one that matters.\n\n**References.** Ahmed, A. (2021), *Evidential Decision Theory*, CUP · Gibbard, A. & Harper, W.L. (1976), \"Counterfactuals and Two Kinds of Expected Utility\" · Wolpert, D. & Benford, G. (2013), \"The lesson of Newcomb's paradox,\" *Synthese* · Greene, P. & Levinstein, B. (2020), \"Act Consequentialism without Free Rides,\" *Philosophical Perspectives* · Conitzer, V. (2015), \"A Dutch book against sleeping beauties who are evidential decision theorists,\" *Synthese* · MacAskill, W. et al. (2021), \"The Evidentialist's Wager\" · Lewis, D. (1981), \"Causal Decision Theory,\" *AJP* · Joyce, J.M. (1999), *The Foundations of Causal Decision Theory* · Eells, E. (1982), *Rational Decision and Causality*.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"Write a ~2500-word essay on EDT: formal definition, conditioning, Newcomb, twin PD, medical Newcomb (smoking lesion) — the source doesn't cover the smoking lesion, but I should develop it as an open problem, plus counterfactual/conditioning issues (P(O|A) when P(A)=0, \"managing the news\", Yao/Eells treatment), evidential wiggle room, distinguish source claims from my proposals. Conclude.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Write a ~2500-word essay on EDT: formal definition, conditioning, Newcomb, twin PD, medical Newcomb (smoking lesion) — the source doesn't cover the smoking lesion, but I should develop it as an open problem, plus counterfactual/conditioning issues (P(O|A) when P(A)=0, \"managing the news\", Yao/Eells treatment), evidential wiggle room, distinguish source claims from my proposals. Conclude.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":4212,"completion_tokens":4651,"total_tokens":8863,"cost":0.0024581,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4160,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.0024581,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0001326,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0023255},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":96,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}